g at four percent so  you know a real negative interest rate  of two and a half percent we have a lot  more investors funneling into the  housing market just be on that basis  property prices have become one of the  big political issues in canada in  elections in september parties across  the political spectrum ran on promises  to tackle soaring house prices  prime minister justin trudeau was  re-elected partly on a pledge to ban  foreigner home purchasers for two years  but vancouver already has a hefty 20 tax  for buyers from overseas  a lot of the foreign owners are really  they're offshore  you know individuals that already have a  canadian citizenship or they have  permanent residency here uh or they have  family that has you know citizenship  here and so they're not really captured  by the tax across the province of bc  foreign buyers made up just over one  percent of all transactions which is a  tiny tiny amount the rise of house  prices in vancouver in recent years  raises a question  when will it stop  surely it can't go on forever it doesn't  feel sustainable  um but it just continues to move higher  when prices have gone up for 30 years  and you can borrow at two percent um  you're going to continue to get this  overwhelming demand that developers just  aren't able to keep up with and  yeah so yeah it's a tough one i just  don't know how it all plays out  while canadians grapple with whether or  not they've got a property bubble on  their hands their neighbors are worrying  about an altogether different imminent  threat  across the border in the united states  hundreds of thousands of americans are  facing the prospect of losing their  homes particularly tenants in places  like new york as state and government  moratoriums on evictions introduced  during the pandemic come to an end  after all americans working in the  sectors where people were suddenly  losing their jobs like retail and  hospitality were far more likely to be  renting than owning their homes  millions of americans have the prospect  of eviction hanging over them in each of  these states over 18 percent of renters  failed to pay their rent on time in  august 2021  in many more states that was still the  case for 10 to 18 percent of tenants  only a few remaining states had a lower  rent delinquency rate  recruit is the strongest i've ever seen  it in history you know so  that makes it very very difficult for  people you know that are kind of living  on the edge so if they're paying  you know  650 bucks a month you know for rent and  that's going up 10 15 20 their incomes  certainly aren't going up 10 15 20  percent so how in a wealthy developed  nation like the united states are so  many people on the verge of being thrown  out onto the street especially when the  federal and state governments have made  available more than enough money to bail  every single one of them out the us  federal government has appropriated uh  almost 50 billion dollars to help  renters with back rent utility payments  uh late fees that kind of thing  and by my calculation uh there are  probably uh  uh about 20 billion dollars 25 billion  dollars in  uh current or marriages so there's  plenty of money  it's just getting that money into the  right hands  one of the barriers to getting the money  quickly is the need to prove that you  lost your job because of the pandemic  marx andy is confident the majority of  people will manage to get the money they  need in time but others are still likely  to fall through the cracks  in a kind of a typical year uh you get  about a million rental evictions so  we'll probably get you know 800k  over the next six months so it'll be a  bit elevated we're going to see it and  it's going to be in certain parts of the  country not everywhere it's not a  problem you know from coast to coast  it's a problem in big urban centers  next we're crossing the atlantic to  dublin where tenants are protecting  themselves by putting up a united front  it is a lot harder for a landlord to  ignore calls from  1300 members across ireland we will  make your life difficult for one for a  better phrase like other cities we've  already looked at dublin's housing  market is red hot but for a unique set  of reasons in dublin a big problem we've  had is lots of developments going up  that are  exclusively built to rent  at a very high profit margin  um looking to cram as much  spaces  into a single building as they can  and then charging premium  ireland's low corporation tax in  comparison with its eu neighbours has  also attracted global tech firms to set  up shop there bringing skilled workers  with the means to pay higher rents  despite attempts to control them for  over a decade now rents across ireland  have been rising and rising but those  risers have been far outstripped by  rents in the capital a place in dublin  city centre will now cost you well over  double what it would have in 2010.  as a result tenants organizations like  cartoon have formed to give individual  tenants the power of the collective they  work like a trade union but for renters  and they've shown they're willing to go  to extreme lengths to protect their  members even blocking evictions while  they're happening  a gentleman had his door drilled in  in the early hours of the morning and a  group of heavies through his entire life  out on the streets a rather intense  standoff with their heavies led to us  essentially shoving them back out of his  house and eventually forcing the  landlord to come down and negotiate with  him and he got to stay in that house  until he now is in more secure housing  but the eviction  was not pushed through the solution to  dublin's problems activists say is  simple build more homes and that is what  the irish government is planning 33 000  of them every year through to 2030 it  should make a major difference  people don't have homes right now people  don't have anywhere to go right now  people are trapped in renting right  now this problem isn't going away this  problem will be worse in 10 years than  it is now  so far we've talked a lot about housing  shortages but building too many houses  can also lead to its own problems the  citizens in our next stop will tell you  in a sense tokyo's housing market is a  story of success despite being a major  city it's remained all but immune to the  high house prices and rising rents that  we've seen in other cities  why  because there are plenty of places to  live  japan has consistently built the best  part of a million new places to live  each year for the past decade the vast  majority of them in cities like tokyo  supply is far outstripping demand in a  country where the population is actually  shrinking  in other words japan has too many homes  and the phenomenon of young japanese  moving out of the countryside to seek  the excitement of the city is resulting  in a real problem  outside of its cities japan is becoming  riddled with abandoned houses many of  them left to rot by owners for whom the  costs and admin associated with keeping  them outweigh any benefits from  maintaining them some researchers say  abandoned homes could account for as  much as 30 percent of the country's  housing stock by 2038. that's a super  abundance of empty houses that many  countries could only dream of  [Music]  and so ends our tour around the world's  troubled housing markets you see a roof  over your head really isn't something  you can take for granted and a global  housing market built  on shaky foundations is one we need to  keep an eye on and that's all from this  edition of business beyond thanks a lot  for watching if you want to know more  about the money that makes the world go  around be sure to check out the business  beyond playlist oh while you're here why  not hit like and subscribe until next  time  take  [Music]


